WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.253
Welcome to Week Twelve of CS104.

2
00:00:03.253 --> 00:00:09.758
This lecture, titled Counting, asks how many possibilities exist when listing them

3
00:00:09.758 --> 00:00:14.095
one by one would be slow or impossible.

4
00:00:14.095 --> 00:00:20.600
We begin with the number of integers in an interval and the

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.853
multiplication rule for sequences of choices.

6
00:00:23.853 --> 00:00:30.358
Then we count ordered arrangements, combine and subtract sets, and choose unordered

7
00:00:30.358 --> 00:00:30.900
groups.

8
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.321
We finish with repeated objects and selections that allow repetition.

9
00:00:36.321 --> 00:00:42.284
Every formula has assumptions about order, replacement, and whether cases overlap.

10
00:00:42.284 --> 00:00:48.789
Before calculating, identify what a single outcome looks like and which outcomes

11
00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:51.500
must be counted exactly once.

12
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.442
Here is the route through Lecture Ten.

13
00:00:55.442 --> 00:01:02.200
Simple Counting begins with a list of consecutive integers and the multiplication

14
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.453
rule for several steps.

15
00:01:04.453 --> 00:01:11.211
Counting on Sets introduces permutations and the addition, difference, and inclusion-exclusion rules.

16
00:01:11.211 --> 00:01:17.968
Combinations count subsets where order is irrelevant; we will also handle indistinguishable

17
00:01:17.968 --> 00:01:21.911
repeated letters and selections with repetition allowed.

18
00:01:21.911 --> 00:01:26.979
The final table brings four common selection formulas together.

19
00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:33.737
The same problem can often be attacked by more than one rule,

20
00:01:33.737 --> 00:01:40.495
but the rules must fit the structure of the objects being counted.

21
00:01:40.495 --> 00:01:45.000
We will explain those choices in worked examples.

22
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.878
Section ten point one begins with the most direct counting task: count

23
00:01:50.878 --> 00:01:53.327
the elements of a list.

24
00:01:53.327 --> 00:01:59.204
Consecutive integers are a useful case because there is a short formula

25
00:01:59.204 --> 00:02:04.102
even when the interval is too long to write out.

26
00:02:04.102 --> 00:02:08.510
The key detail is whether both endpoints are included.

27
00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:14.388
Once we can count an integer interval, we can count items whose

28
00:02:14.388 --> 00:02:20.265
labels run through such an interval, such as the multipliers producing three-digit

29
00:02:20.265 --> 00:02:21.735
multiples of five.

30
00:02:21.735 --> 00:02:27.612
After that, the lecture turns from one list to a sequence of

31
00:02:27.612 --> 00:02:33.000
choices, where the multiplication rule can replace a much larger enumeration.

32
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.429
Look at the integers from five through twelve.

33
00:02:36.429 --> 00:02:41.571
The slide pairs five with count one, six with count two, and

34
00:02:41.571 --> 00:02:45.429
so on until twelve is paired with count eight.

35
00:02:45.429 --> 00:02:50.571
Subtracting twelve minus five gives seven gaps between endpoints, but there are

36
00:02:50.571 --> 00:02:53.571
eight listed integers because both endpoints count.

37
00:02:53.571 --> 00:02:58.714
In general, when m and n are integers with m at most

38
00:02:58.714 --> 00:03:03.857
n, the inclusive list m, m plus one, through n has n

39
00:03:03.857 --> 00:03:06.000
minus m plus one elements.

40
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.143
One way to see this is to shift every item down by

41
00:03:11.143 --> 00:03:16.286
m: the list becomes zero through n minus m, which has one

42
00:03:16.286 --> 00:03:20.143
item for zero plus one for each positive value.

43
00:03:20.143 --> 00:03:25.286
Test the formula with m equal to n; the answer is one,

44
00:03:25.286 --> 00:03:27.000
as it should be.

45
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.333
How many three-digit integers are divisible by five?

46
00:03:31.333 --> 00:03:36.750
The first is one hundred, which is five times twenty.

47
00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:43.250
The last is nine hundred ninety-five, which is five times one hundred

48
00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:43.792
ninety-nine.

49
00:03:43.792 --> 00:03:50.292
Every multiple in between has exactly one integer multiplier from twenty through

50
00:03:50.292 --> 00:03:56.792
one hundred ninety-nine, and every multiplier in that interval produces exactly one

51
00:03:56.792 --> 00:03:58.417
eligible three-digit number.

52
00:03:58.417 --> 00:04:03.833
So we count the multipliers rather than writing every multiple.

53
00:04:03.833 --> 00:04:10.333
The inclusive-list rule gives one hundred ninety-nine minus twenty plus one, or

54
00:04:10.333 --> 00:04:11.958
one hundred eighty.

55
00:04:11.958 --> 00:04:18.458
Using nine hundred ninety-nine as though it were divisible by five, or

56
00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:24.958
forgetting the plus one for the inclusive endpoints, would give the wrong

57
00:04:24.958 --> 00:04:25.500
answer.

58
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:31.896
Now count possible four-symbol PINs when each position may contain any of

59
00:04:31.896 --> 00:04:34.561
twenty-six letters or ten digits.

60
00:04:34.561 --> 00:04:38.825
That makes thirty-six available symbols for each position.

61
00:04:38.825 --> 00:04:45.222
The slide shows a choice at each of four successive positions, and

62
00:04:45.222 --> 00:04:50.552
repetition is allowed: a symbol used earlier remains available later.

63
00:04:50.552 --> 00:04:56.948
The sample PINs include letters only, digits only, and mixtures, so do

64
00:04:56.948 --> 00:05:03.344
not impose an extra condition that every PIN must contain both types.

65
00:05:03.344 --> 00:05:08.675
The multiplication rule will multiply the four numbers of choices.

66
00:05:08.675 --> 00:05:15.071
Because each position still has thirty-six choices, the total is thirty-six to

67
00:05:15.071 --> 00:05:21.467
the fourth power, or one million six hundred seventy-nine thousand six hundred

68
00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:22.000
sixteen.

69
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.150
The multiplication rule formalizes a sequence of choices.

70
00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:32.374
If step one has n one choices, step two has n two

71
00:05:32.374 --> 00:05:38.598
choices regardless of which first choice was made, and the same fixed-count

72
00:05:38.598 --> 00:05:43.266
condition holds for each later step, multiply the counts.

73
00:05:43.266 --> 00:05:49.491
For the four-symbol PIN, that is thirty-six times thirty-six times thirty-six times

74
00:05:49.491 --> 00:05:50.009
thirty-six.

75
00:05:50.009 --> 00:05:54.159
The phrase regardless of earlier choices is essential.

76
00:05:54.159 --> 00:06:00.383
The individual options can change, but the number of options must remain

77
00:06:00.383 --> 00:06:06.089
fixed across every earlier branch for this simple product to work.

78
00:06:06.089 --> 00:06:12.313
If a later step has a different number of choices in different

79
00:06:12.313 --> 00:06:17.500
branches, split the cases or reorder the steps before multiplying.

80
00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.472
The officer problem tests that condition.

81
00:06:20.472 --> 00:06:26.416
Choose a president, treasurer, and secretary from Ann, Bob, Cyd, and Dan,

82
00:06:26.416 --> 00:06:27.902
using distinct people.

83
00:06:27.902 --> 00:06:33.846
Ann cannot be president, and the secretary must be Cyd or Dan.

84
00:06:33.846 --> 00:06:39.790
A tempting product says three choices for president, three for treasurer, and

85
00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:42.266
two for secretary, giving eighteen.

86
00:06:42.266 --> 00:06:43.752
That is incorrect.

87
00:06:43.752 --> 00:06:49.696
If Cyd or Dan has already taken an earlier office, fewer eligible

88
00:06:49.696 --> 00:06:53.659
secretaries remain; in one branch, neither may remain.

89
00:06:53.659 --> 00:06:59.603
The count of secretary options depends on the earlier choices, so it

90
00:06:59.603 --> 00:07:05.547
cannot be represented by a fixed factor of two at the end.

91
00:07:05.547 --> 00:07:10.500
We need to inspect branches or choose a better order.

92
00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:13.968
The tree diagram makes the dependency visible.

93
00:07:13.968 --> 00:07:18.923
The first branches choose Bob, Cyd, or Dan as president.

94
00:07:18.923 --> 00:07:24.868
Later branches choose a different treasurer, and only the paths that end

95
00:07:24.868 --> 00:07:28.832
with Cyd or Dan as secretary are valid.

96
00:07:28.832 --> 00:07:34.777
Instead of multiplying one count per column, trace each complete path that

97
00:07:34.777 --> 00:07:36.264
obeys every restriction.

98
00:07:36.264 --> 00:07:39.236
The diagram shows eight such outcomes.

99
00:07:39.236 --> 00:07:45.182
For example, if Bob is president and Ann is treasurer, either Cyd

100
00:07:45.182 --> 00:07:47.659
or Dan can be secretary.

101
00:07:47.659 --> 00:07:53.605
But if Cyd is president and Dan is treasurer, there is no

102
00:07:53.605 --> 00:07:55.091
eligible secretary left.

103
00:07:55.091 --> 00:08:01.036
This is why the earlier multiplication gave eighteen: it counted impossible final

104
00:08:01.036 --> 00:08:05.000
choices as if they existed on every branch.

105
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.550
There is a cleaner solution: choose the most restricted office first.

106
00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:14.587
The secretary has two choices, Cyd or Dan.

107
00:08:14.587 --> 00:08:20.642
Once the secretary is fixed, the president cannot be Ann and cannot

108
00:08:20.642 --> 00:08:26.193
be the chosen secretary, leaving two choices among the other people.

109
00:08:26.193 --> 00:08:31.239
Then two of the original four remain available for treasurer.

110
00:08:31.239 --> 00:08:37.294
Now the number of choices at each step is constant across all

111
00:08:37.294 --> 00:08:43.349
previous branches, so the multiplication rule gives two times two times two,

112
00:08:43.349 --> 00:08:44.358
or eight.

113
00:08:44.358 --> 00:08:50.413
Reordering steps does not change the final assignment being counted; it changes

114
00:08:50.413 --> 00:08:53.945
only the way we describe its construction.

115
00:08:53.945 --> 00:09:00.000
This is a useful tactic whenever constraints make a naive product unreliable.

116
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.815
Section ten point two shifts to counting on sets.

117
00:09:04.815 --> 00:09:10.700
First we count permutations, which are complete orderings of distinct objects.

118
00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:17.120
Later, set operations help us combine, subtract, and correct for overlapping collections

119
00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:18.190
of outcomes.

120
00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:24.610
The distinction between an ordered outcome and a subset is worth keeping

121
00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:31.030
in view: a row of officers or letters has positions, while a

122
00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:33.705
team is merely a group.

123
00:09:33.705 --> 00:09:40.125
In the next slides, every object in the starting set is distinct

124
00:09:40.125 --> 00:09:43.335
unless the lecture explicitly says otherwise.

125
00:09:43.335 --> 00:09:49.755
We will derive factorial and partial-permutation formulas from the multiplication rule before

126
00:09:49.755 --> 00:09:53.500
using set operations for other counting problems.

127
00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:58.976
A permutation of a set is an ordering of its objects in

128
00:09:58.976 --> 00:09:59.888
a row.

129
00:09:59.888 --> 00:10:05.364
The three distinct letters a, b, and c have six possible rows:

130
00:10:05.364 --> 00:10:08.558
abc, acb, bac, bca, cab, and cba.

131
00:10:08.558 --> 00:10:14.034
The same three letters appear in every row, but changing their order

132
00:10:14.034 --> 00:10:15.859
creates a different permutation.

133
00:10:15.859 --> 00:10:21.335
For instance, abc and bac are distinct even though they contain the

134
00:10:21.335 --> 00:10:23.160
same set of letters.

135
00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:28.636
We can list six rows here, but listing becomes impractical as the

136
00:10:28.636 --> 00:10:29.549
set grows.

137
00:10:29.549 --> 00:10:35.024
The natural question is how many rows can be formed from n

138
00:10:35.024 --> 00:10:35.937
distinct objects.

139
00:10:35.937 --> 00:10:40.500
We will answer by filling positions from left to right.

140
00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:46.014
To arrange n distinct objects, choose the first position in n ways.

141
00:10:46.014 --> 00:10:51.527
After using one object, choose the second in n minus one ways.

142
00:10:51.527 --> 00:10:57.041
The third has n minus two choices, and the final position has

143
00:10:57.041 --> 00:10:57.500
one.

144
00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:03.014
Every full row arises from exactly one such sequence of choices, so

145
00:11:03.014 --> 00:11:07.149
multiply n times n minus one down to one.

146
00:11:07.149 --> 00:11:11.284
That product is n factorial, written n exclamation mark.

147
00:11:11.284 --> 00:11:15.878
The slide states the theorem for n at least one.

148
00:11:15.878 --> 00:11:21.392
For three letters it gives three times two times one, or six,

149
00:11:21.392 --> 00:11:22.770
matching the list.

150
00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:28.284
Notice how the number of available objects decreases because repetition is not

151
00:11:28.284 --> 00:11:31.500
allowed in a permutation of a set.

152
00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:37.389
The word COMPUTER has eight distinct letters, so all arrangements use eight

153
00:11:37.389 --> 00:11:41.315
factorial, or forty thousand three hundred twenty, rows.

154
00:11:41.315 --> 00:11:47.204
If the letters C and O must stay together in that order,

155
00:11:47.204 --> 00:11:53.093
treat the fixed block CO as one object along with the six

156
00:11:53.093 --> 00:11:54.074
other letters.

157
00:11:54.074 --> 00:11:59.963
There are then seven distinct objects to arrange, giving seven factorial, or

158
00:11:59.963 --> 00:12:01.435
five thousand forty.

159
00:12:01.435 --> 00:12:06.833
The words in that order matter: the block is CO only.

160
00:12:06.833 --> 00:12:12.722
If either CO or OC were allowed, that would be a different

161
00:12:12.722 --> 00:12:16.157
problem with an extra factor of two.

162
00:12:16.157 --> 00:12:22.046
The block method works because every admissible arrangement corresponds to exactly one

163
00:12:22.046 --> 00:12:24.500
arrangement of the seven objects.

164
00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:29.577
We can also select only r of n distinct objects and arrange

165
00:12:29.577 --> 00:12:30.846
those selected objects.

166
00:12:30.846 --> 00:12:35.923
For the set containing a, b, and c, the ordered pairs are

167
00:12:35.923 --> 00:12:38.885
ab, ac, ba, bc, ca, and cb.

168
00:12:38.885 --> 00:12:42.692
This is a two-permutation, and ab differs from ba.

169
00:12:42.692 --> 00:12:47.769
Fill the first position in n ways, the second in n minus

170
00:12:47.769 --> 00:12:50.731
one ways, and stop after r positions.

171
00:12:50.731 --> 00:12:55.808
The product is n times n minus one down to n minus

172
00:12:55.808 --> 00:13:00.885
r plus one, or n factorial divided by the factorial of n

173
00:13:00.885 --> 00:13:01.731
minus r.

174
00:13:01.731 --> 00:13:06.808
The condition is one at most r at most n on this

175
00:13:06.808 --> 00:13:07.231
slide.

176
00:13:07.231 --> 00:13:12.308
Unlike a combination, an r-permutation keeps track of the order in which

177
00:13:12.308 --> 00:13:14.000
the chosen elements appear.

178
00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:17.309
Use the formula in three small examples.

179
00:13:17.309 --> 00:13:22.982
P of five comma two is five times four, or twenty, because

180
00:13:22.982 --> 00:13:27.236
we fill two ordered positions from five distinct objects.

181
00:13:27.236 --> 00:13:32.909
P of seven comma four is seven times six times five times

182
00:13:32.909 --> 00:13:35.273
four, or eight hundred forty.

183
00:13:35.273 --> 00:13:40.945
P of five comma five uses every object and equals five factorial,

184
00:13:40.945 --> 00:13:42.836
or one hundred twenty.

185
00:13:42.836 --> 00:13:48.509
In the factorial fraction, the final example has zero factorial in the

186
00:13:48.509 --> 00:13:54.182
denominator; zero factorial is one, so the expression agrees with a full

187
00:13:54.182 --> 00:13:54.655
permutation.

188
00:13:54.655 --> 00:14:00.327
These examples also show why the product stops after r factors rather

189
00:14:00.327 --> 00:14:06.000
than multiplying all the way down to one unless r equals n.

190
00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:09.571
Sometimes the outcomes fall into separate categories.

191
00:14:09.571 --> 00:14:15.694
If sets A one, A two, and so on are pairwise disjoint,

192
00:14:15.694 --> 00:14:21.816
no outcome belongs to two categories, and the size of their union

193
00:14:21.816 --> 00:14:24.878
is the sum of their sizes.

194
00:14:24.878 --> 00:14:30.490
Pairwise disjoint means every intersection of two different categories is empty.

195
00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:34.061
The slide calls this the addition rule.

196
00:14:34.061 --> 00:14:36.612
The condition prevents double counting.

197
00:14:36.612 --> 00:14:42.735
If an outcome can appear in more than one category, adding category

198
00:14:42.735 --> 00:14:48.857
sizes counts it repeatedly, and we need a correction such as inclusion-exclusion.

199
00:14:48.857 --> 00:14:54.980
The next example works because PINs of different lengths cannot be the

200
00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:56.000
same sequence.

201
00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:02.436
A PIN of one to three letters may have length one, length

202
00:15:02.436 --> 00:15:06.727
two, or length three, and letters may repeat.

203
00:15:06.727 --> 00:15:09.945
Those three length categories are disjoint.

204
00:15:09.945 --> 00:15:16.382
A one-letter PIN has twenty-six choices; a two-letter PIN has twenty-six squared;

205
00:15:16.382 --> 00:15:20.136
and a three-letter PIN has twenty-six cubed.

206
00:15:20.136 --> 00:15:26.573
The multiplication rule counts within each length, and the addition rule combines

207
00:15:26.573 --> 00:15:33.009
the lengths: twenty-six plus six hundred seventy-six plus seventeen thousand five hundred

208
00:15:33.009 --> 00:15:36.764
seventy-six equals eighteen thousand two hundred seventy-eight.

209
00:15:36.764 --> 00:15:43.200
Do not multiply the three length totals together, because a PIN has

210
00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:48.027
one chosen length, not all three lengths at once.

211
00:15:48.027 --> 00:15:54.464
Nor should the empty sequence be included; the permitted lengths start at

212
00:15:54.464 --> 00:15:55.000
one.

213
00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:59.856
The difference rule counts what remains after excluding a known subset.

214
00:15:59.856 --> 00:16:05.153
If B is contained in a finite set A, then the number

215
00:16:05.153 --> 00:16:10.450
in A but not B is the size of A minus the

216
00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:11.775
size of B.

217
00:16:11.775 --> 00:16:16.189
This is often easier than counting a complicated target directly.

218
00:16:16.189 --> 00:16:21.486
To count objects with at least one special feature, let A be

219
00:16:21.486 --> 00:16:26.784
all objects and B be the objects with none of that feature.

220
00:16:26.784 --> 00:16:29.432
The target is A minus B.

221
00:16:29.432 --> 00:16:34.730
We will apply this to four-symbol PINs with at least one repeated

222
00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:35.171
symbol.

223
00:16:35.171 --> 00:16:40.468
Be precise about B: it must be a subset of A so

224
00:16:40.468 --> 00:16:44.000
that subtraction removes exactly the unwanted outcomes once.

225
00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:49.347
Return to four-symbol PINs from thirty-six letters and digits.

226
00:16:49.347 --> 00:16:56.475
With repetition allowed, there are thirty-six to the fourth power, or one

227
00:16:56.475 --> 00:17:02.416
million six hundred seventy-nine thousand six hundred sixteen total PINs.

228
00:17:02.416 --> 00:17:09.545
The complement of having at least one repeated symbol is having four

229
00:17:09.545 --> 00:17:10.733
distinct symbols.

230
00:17:10.733 --> 00:17:17.861
Fill the four positions without replacement: thirty-six times thirty-five times thirty-four times

231
00:17:17.861 --> 00:17:24.396
thirty-three, or one million four hundred thirteen thousand seven hundred twenty.

232
00:17:24.396 --> 00:17:31.525
Subtracting gives two hundred sixty-five thousand eight hundred ninety-six PINs with at

233
00:17:31.525 --> 00:17:33.307
least one repeat.

234
00:17:33.307 --> 00:17:40.436
This includes PINs with several repeated positions; the complement method handles all

235
00:17:40.436 --> 00:17:44.000
such patterns without making overlapping cases.

236
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:50.178
When sets overlap, simply adding their sizes counts shared elements more than

237
00:17:50.178 --> 00:17:50.693
once.

238
00:17:50.693 --> 00:17:56.871
For two sets, add the sizes of A and B, then subtract

239
00:17:56.871 --> 00:17:59.960
the size of their intersection once.

240
00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:06.139
For three sets, add the three individual sizes, subtract each pairwise intersection,

241
00:18:06.139 --> 00:18:09.228
then add the triple intersection back.

242
00:18:09.228 --> 00:18:15.406
A triple-overlap element was counted three times in the first sum and

243
00:18:15.406 --> 00:18:21.584
subtracted three times in the pairwise correction, so adding it once gives

244
00:18:21.584 --> 00:18:23.644
the correct final count.

245
00:18:23.644 --> 00:18:27.248
The Venn diagram illustrates the shared region.

246
00:18:27.248 --> 00:18:33.426
The formulas count elements in the union, meaning outcomes satisfying at least

247
00:18:33.426 --> 00:18:36.000
one of the listed conditions.

248
00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:42.340
Count integers from one through one thousand that are multiples of three

249
00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:43.396
or five.

250
00:18:43.396 --> 00:18:49.736
Set A contains multiples of three, so its size is the integer

251
00:18:49.736 --> 00:18:55.547
part of one thousand divided by three, or three hundred thirty-three.

252
00:18:55.547 --> 00:19:00.302
Set B contains multiples of five, giving two hundred.

253
00:19:00.302 --> 00:19:06.642
Their intersection contains multiples of both, which are multiples of fifteen, giving

254
00:19:06.642 --> 00:19:07.170
sixty-six.

255
00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:13.509
Inclusion-exclusion gives three hundred thirty-three plus two hundred minus sixty-six, or four

256
00:19:13.509 --> 00:19:14.566
hundred sixty-seven.

257
00:19:14.566 --> 00:19:20.906
Subtract the overlap because those sixty-six integers were counted in both A

258
00:19:20.906 --> 00:19:21.962
and B.

259
00:19:21.962 --> 00:19:28.302
The word or is inclusive here: multiples of fifteen qualify, but they

260
00:19:28.302 --> 00:19:32.000
must appear once in the final count.

261
00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:35.857
Section ten point three turns to combinations.

262
00:19:35.857 --> 00:19:42.469
A combination selects a subset of a given size from a set

263
00:19:42.469 --> 00:19:44.122
of distinct objects.

264
00:19:44.122 --> 00:19:50.184
The order of selected objects does not create a new subset.

265
00:19:50.184 --> 00:19:56.796
We will derive the choose formula by comparing combinations with ordered selections,

266
00:19:56.796 --> 00:19:59.551
then apply it to teams.

267
00:19:59.551 --> 00:20:06.163
The section also treats indistinguishable repeated objects, binomial coefficients, and combinations where

268
00:20:06.163 --> 00:20:11.122
choosing the same category more than once is allowed.

269
00:20:11.122 --> 00:20:17.735
The words combination and repetition describe different aspects of a problem: combination

270
00:20:17.735 --> 00:20:24.347
concerns whether order matters, while repetition concerns whether a selected type can

271
00:20:24.347 --> 00:20:26.000
be used again.

272
00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:32.057
Given a set S with n distinct elements, an r-combination is a

273
00:20:32.057 --> 00:20:34.580
subset containing exactly r elements.

274
00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:40.637
The notation n choose r counts those subsets for nonnegative integers n

275
00:20:40.637 --> 00:20:44.675
and r with r no larger than n.

276
00:20:44.675 --> 00:20:50.731
For example, from the set containing one, two, and three, the two-element

277
00:20:50.731 --> 00:20:53.759
subsets are one-two, one-three, and two-three.

278
00:20:53.759 --> 00:20:59.816
Writing two-one does not add another subset; the set of members is

279
00:20:59.816 --> 00:21:00.321
unchanged.

280
00:21:00.321 --> 00:21:06.377
This is the central difference from the six ordered two-permutations on the

281
00:21:06.377 --> 00:21:07.387
previous slides.

282
00:21:07.387 --> 00:21:13.443
We have defined what is being counted, but not yet derived the

283
00:21:13.443 --> 00:21:14.453
numerical formula.

284
00:21:14.453 --> 00:21:19.500
That comes by relating each subset to its possible orderings.

285
00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:25.174
The slide compares ordered and unordered choices side by side.

286
00:21:25.174 --> 00:21:31.983
From one, two, and three, the ordered two-selections are one-two, two-one, one-three,

287
00:21:31.983 --> 00:21:35.388
three-one, two-three, and three-two: six outcomes.

288
00:21:35.388 --> 00:21:42.197
The unordered two-element subsets are only one-two, one-three, and two-three: three outcomes.

289
00:21:42.197 --> 00:21:47.871
Each subset corresponds to exactly two factorial, or two, orderings.

290
00:21:47.871 --> 00:21:54.680
This suggests a general relationship: if we first choose an r-element subset

291
00:21:54.680 --> 00:22:01.489
and then order its r distinct members, we obtain every r-permutation exactly

292
00:22:01.489 --> 00:22:02.056
once.

293
00:22:02.056 --> 00:22:08.865
The next slide converts that two-step process into the formula for n

294
00:22:08.865 --> 00:22:10.000
choose r.

295
00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.259
Build an ordered r-selection in two steps.

296
00:22:13.259 --> 00:22:17.914
First choose an r-element subset, in n choose r ways.

297
00:22:17.914 --> 00:22:22.569
Then order its r distinct elements, in r factorial ways.

298
00:22:22.569 --> 00:22:28.155
The multiplication rule says P of n comma r equals n choose

299
00:22:28.155 --> 00:22:30.017
r times r factorial.

300
00:22:30.017 --> 00:22:35.603
Divide to get n choose r equals P of n comma r

301
00:22:35.603 --> 00:22:41.190
over r factorial, or n factorial over r factorial times n minus

302
00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:42.121
r factorial.

303
00:22:42.121 --> 00:22:47.707
The division removes the repeated counting caused by different orders of the

304
00:22:47.707 --> 00:22:48.638
same subset.

305
00:22:48.638 --> 00:22:54.224
It is not appropriate when the outcome itself has ordered roles; for

306
00:22:54.224 --> 00:22:58.879
officers, a president and secretary swapped is a different assignment.

307
00:22:58.879 --> 00:23:04.000
The formula also gives one empty subset when r equals zero.

308
00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:08.458
A twelve-person group contains five boys and seven girls.

309
00:23:08.458 --> 00:23:13.907
How many five-person teams have exactly three boys and two girls?

310
00:23:13.907 --> 00:23:19.850
First choose three of the five boys, in five choose three ways.

311
00:23:19.850 --> 00:23:25.794
Independently choose two of the seven girls, in seven choose two ways.

312
00:23:25.794 --> 00:23:31.738
Multiply because every final team corresponds to exactly one pair of choices,

313
00:23:31.738 --> 00:23:33.720
one from each category.

314
00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:39.664
Five choose three is ten and seven choose two is twenty-one, giving

315
00:23:39.664 --> 00:23:41.645
two hundred ten teams.

316
00:23:41.645 --> 00:23:47.589
We do not multiply by five factorial: a team has no internal

317
00:23:47.589 --> 00:23:49.570
order or assigned positions.

318
00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:55.514
Here we are making both choices, so multiplication rather than addition is

319
00:23:55.514 --> 00:23:57.000
the right operation.

320
00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:01.900
Now ask for five-person teams with at least one boy.

321
00:24:01.900 --> 00:24:07.780
Counting separately the teams with exactly one, two, three, four, and five

322
00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:11.700
boys would work, but it creates five cases.

323
00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:13.660
The complement is simpler.

324
00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:19.540
There are twelve choose five total teams, which is seven hundred ninety-two.

325
00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:25.420
The teams with no boy use five of the seven girls, giving

326
00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:27.870
seven choose five, or twenty-one.

327
00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:33.750
Subtract to obtain seven hundred seventy-one teams with at least one boy.

328
00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:39.630
The phrase at least one signals that the zero-boy case is the

329
00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:41.100
one to remove.

330
00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:46.000
This is the difference rule combined with the combination formula.

331
00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:50.000
The letters of MISSISSIPPI are not all distinct.

332
00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:56.000
There are eleven positions, four S letters, four I letters, two P

333
00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:58.000
letters, and one M.

334
00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:04.000
One constructive count chooses four positions for the S letters, then four

335
00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:10.000
of the remaining seven for I, then two of the remaining three

336
00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:13.000
for P, leaving one for M.

337
00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:19.000
The product of eleven choose four, seven choose four, three choose two,

338
00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:24.000
and one choose one is thirty-four thousand six hundred fifty.

339
00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:30.000
If we started with eleven factorial, we would overcount exchanges among identical

340
00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:34.500
S letters, identical I letters, and identical P letters.

341
00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:40.500
Dividing by four factorial, four factorial, and two factorial yields the same

342
00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:41.000
answer.

343
00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:44.689
The general repeated-object theorem extends that reasoning.

344
00:25:44.689 --> 00:25:51.014
Suppose n positions are filled with n one indistinguishable objects of type

345
00:25:51.014 --> 00:25:57.338
one, n two of type two, and so on, with the group

346
00:25:57.338 --> 00:25:58.919
sizes totaling n.

347
00:25:58.919 --> 00:26:05.243
The number of distinguishable arrangements is n factorial divided by the product

348
00:26:05.243 --> 00:26:11.568
of n one factorial, n two factorial, and the other group factorials.

349
00:26:11.568 --> 00:26:17.892
Each swap among objects of the same type leaves the visible arrangement

350
00:26:17.892 --> 00:26:23.162
unchanged, so the ordinary n factorial count includes many duplicates.

351
00:26:23.162 --> 00:26:28.959
An equivalent constructive method chooses positions for each type in sequence.

352
00:26:28.959 --> 00:26:35.284
Before using this formula, confirm that objects within each type truly are

353
00:26:35.284 --> 00:26:39.500
indistinguishable and that all n objects are used.

354
00:26:39.500 --> 00:26:42.671
The binomial expansion has a counting interpretation.

355
00:26:42.671 --> 00:26:48.107
In the product of n copies of a plus b, each term

356
00:26:48.107 --> 00:26:52.637
arises by choosing either a or b from each copy.

357
00:26:52.637 --> 00:26:58.073
To obtain a to the power n minus k times b to

358
00:26:58.073 --> 00:27:03.509
the power k, choose exactly k of the n copies from which

359
00:27:03.509 --> 00:27:07.132
to take b; all remaining copies contribute a.

360
00:27:07.132 --> 00:27:11.209
The number of such choices is n choose k.

361
00:27:11.209 --> 00:27:16.645
That explains the coefficient in the binomial theorem, rather than treating it

362
00:27:16.645 --> 00:27:18.910
as an unexplained algebraic number.

363
00:27:18.910 --> 00:27:24.346
We are choosing a subset of positions, so their order is irrelevant.

364
00:27:24.346 --> 00:27:29.782
Each choice of k positions produces the same monomial, and the coefficient

365
00:27:29.782 --> 00:27:32.500
counts how many choices do so.

366
00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:39.100
Now allow the same category to be selected more than once, while

367
00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:42.400
the order of selections remains irrelevant.

368
00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:49.000
From categories one, two, three, and four, the slide lists all size-three

369
00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:49.550
multisets.

370
00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:52.850
Examples include one-one-one, one-one-two, and one-three-four.

371
00:27:52.850 --> 00:27:55.600
The list contains twenty outcomes.

372
00:27:55.600 --> 00:28:02.200
There are four cubed ordered triples, but many of those triples represent

373
00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:07.700
the same multiset, so four cubed is not the answer.

374
00:28:07.700 --> 00:28:13.200
Nor can we use four choose three, which forbids repetition.

375
00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:19.800
The next slides show a one-to-one correspondence between these multisets and arrangements

376
00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:22.000
of stars and separators.

377
00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:26.661
Think of the four labels as four fixed categories.

378
00:28:26.661 --> 00:28:32.875
A star means one item selected from a category, and three vertical

379
00:28:32.875 --> 00:28:35.464
bars separate the four categories.

380
00:28:35.464 --> 00:28:40.643
For example, three stars before the first bar represents one-one-one.

381
00:28:40.643 --> 00:28:46.857
A star in the first compartment, one in the third, and one

382
00:28:46.857 --> 00:28:49.446
in the fourth represents one-three-four.

383
00:28:49.446 --> 00:28:55.661
The encoding uses three stars and three bars, six symbols in total.

384
00:28:55.661 --> 00:29:01.875
Choose which three of the six positions hold stars; the remaining positions

385
00:29:01.875 --> 00:29:02.911
hold bars.

386
00:29:02.911 --> 00:29:09.125
That gives six choose three, or twenty, encodings and therefore twenty size-three

387
00:29:09.125 --> 00:29:09.643
multisets.

388
00:29:09.643 --> 00:29:15.857
This is the stars-and-bars idea: unlike an ordered triple, the notation records

389
00:29:15.857 --> 00:29:20.000
only how many objects came from each category.

390
00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:23.108
The theorem generalizes the previous picture.

391
00:29:23.108 --> 00:29:29.324
To choose r items from n categories with repetitions allowed and order

392
00:29:29.324 --> 00:29:35.541
ignored, use r stars for the chosen items and n minus one

393
00:29:35.541 --> 00:29:37.613
separators between the categories.

394
00:29:37.613 --> 00:29:42.275
The row has r plus n minus one positions.

395
00:29:42.275 --> 00:29:48.491
Choosing the r star positions gives r plus n minus one choose

396
00:29:48.491 --> 00:29:49.527
r outcomes.

397
00:29:49.527 --> 00:29:52.117
Equivalently, choose the separator positions.

398
00:29:52.117 --> 00:29:58.333
For four categories and three items, this becomes six choose three, or

399
00:29:58.333 --> 00:29:58.851
twenty.

400
00:29:58.851 --> 00:30:05.068
The formula applies to categories whose supply is unrestricted; if each category

401
00:30:05.068 --> 00:30:11.284
has a cap, the simple stars-and-bars count may include forbidden selections and

402
00:30:11.284 --> 00:30:12.320
needs correction.

403
00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:17.500
The number n of categories is positive in this construction.

404
00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:23.302
The final table is a decision guide for choosing k items from

405
00:30:23.302 --> 00:30:24.269
n types.

406
00:30:24.269 --> 00:30:26.202
Ask two independent questions.

407
00:30:26.202 --> 00:30:27.653
Does order matter?

408
00:30:27.653 --> 00:30:29.103
Is repetition allowed?

409
00:30:29.103 --> 00:30:34.905
If both answers are yes, fill k positions independently in n to

410
00:30:34.905 --> 00:30:36.355
the k ways.

411
00:30:36.355 --> 00:30:42.157
If order matters but repetition is forbidden, use the falling product P

412
00:30:42.157 --> 00:30:44.091
of n comma k.

413
00:30:44.091 --> 00:30:49.893
If order does not matter and repetition is forbidden, use n choose

414
00:30:49.893 --> 00:30:50.376
k.

415
00:30:50.376 --> 00:30:56.178
If order does not matter and repetition is allowed, use n plus

416
00:30:56.178 --> 00:30:58.595
k minus one choose k.

417
00:30:58.595 --> 00:31:04.397
Check the result against a tiny example before trusting it, and remember

418
00:31:04.397 --> 00:31:10.198
that extra conditions, such as restricted offices or overlapping categories, may require

419
00:31:10.198 --> 00:31:16.000
case splitting, a complement, or inclusion-exclusion on top of the base formula.
